20:14.740 --> 20:19.540
Or that particles are somehow communicating backward in time to know where the other particles are

20:19.540 --> 20:21.640
so they can form a zebra-stripe pattern.

20:22.260 --> 20:25.100
Flyxion is arguing that it is none of those things.

20:25.100 --> 20:28.200
Interference is not physical waves sloshing through the ether.

20:28.540 --> 20:30.780
It is simply compositional memory returning.

20:31.160 --> 20:32.000
Compositional memory.

20:32.180 --> 20:32.380
Yes.

20:32.720 --> 20:38.240
The phase, which is the complex number part of the amplitude that everyone gets so endlessly mystified by,

20:38.420 --> 20:42.100
is not a little physical dial spinning on the back of a tiny electron.

20:42.460 --> 20:46.020
It is purely and simply bookkeeping for continuation.

20:46.500 --> 20:47.740
Bookkeeping for continuation.

20:48.160 --> 20:48.980
I love that phrasing.

20:48.980 --> 20:51.520
The author makes a very strict philosophical point here.

20:52.080 --> 20:55.880
A truly complete description of a physical state must screen off the relevant past.

20:56.120 --> 20:57.940
What does that mean, to screen off the past?

20:58.140 --> 21:03.220
It means that if I hand you the true, complete mathematical state of a system right now,

21:03.840 --> 21:10.320
knowing the entire history of how the system arrived at that state shouldn't help you make better predictions about its future.

21:10.460 --> 21:10.760
Okay, wait.

21:10.820 --> 21:11.260
Let me think.

21:11.260 --> 21:14.720
The current state should contain everything you need.

21:15.020 --> 21:15.660
Ah, okay.

21:16.120 --> 21:20.620
If I know exactly how fast a baseball is moving and exactly where it is right now,

21:20.780 --> 21:23.600
I don't need to know who threw it to predict where it will land.

21:23.800 --> 21:26.460
The current state screens off the history of the pitcher.

21:26.740 --> 21:27.200
Exactly.

21:27.660 --> 21:28.520
And here's the kicker.

21:28.940 --> 21:33.480
A bare probability distribution fails this test entirely.

21:33.640 --> 21:35.200
Because it threw away the ciphertext.

21:35.360 --> 21:35.640
Right.

21:35.640 --> 21:40.560
You can have two quantum systems that had the exact same probability distribution right now.

21:41.580 --> 21:43.300
If you look at their shadows, they are identical.

21:43.620 --> 21:43.700
Yeah.

21:43.840 --> 21:49.060
But because they arrived at that probability via different amplitude paths, different histories,

21:49.360 --> 21:53.440
they will react completely differently to the exact same transformation tomorrow.

21:53.720 --> 21:54.240
Oh, wow.

21:54.420 --> 21:55.980
One might interfere constructively.

21:56.080 --> 21:57.920
The other might interfere destructively.

21:58.240 --> 22:00.840
The probability table does not screen off the past.

22:00.900 --> 22:02.160
But the amplitude state does.

22:02.160 --> 22:05.720
The amplitude state contains the memory of how to compose.

22:06.380 --> 22:07.540
It passes the test.

22:08.160 --> 22:11.520
This completely reframes the entire concept of measurement, doesn't it?

22:11.540 --> 22:14.480
Or what physicists dramatically call wave function collapse.

22:14.660 --> 22:15.280
It really does.

22:15.400 --> 22:20.200
When I learned quantum mechanics, measurement was always presented as this violent, magical event

22:20.200 --> 22:24.580
where the universe suddenly decides to stop being a blurry quantum cloud

22:24.580 --> 22:28.320
and forces itself to be a sharp, classical object.

22:28.640 --> 22:31.800
Flyxion's reconstruction strips away all that mysticism.

22:32.160 --> 22:35.740
Measurement isn't the universe making a physical choice.

22:36.280 --> 22:40.680
Measurement is just a change in which compositional distinctions remain available for future use.

22:41.080 --> 22:41.460
Wow.

22:41.660 --> 22:47.200
So when you record a probability, when you hear the Geiger counter click, you aren't destroying a physical wave.

22:47.600 --> 22:51.400
You are just mathematically discarding the phase relations, the memory,

22:51.760 --> 22:56.060
that would have allowed for coherent composition if you had kept the system isolated.

22:56.060 --> 23:00.600
You're locking in the scoreboard and you're throwing the tape of the game into the incinerator.

23:01.440 --> 23:02.540
The mechanics are gone.

23:02.980 --> 23:04.100
Only the witness remains.

23:04.620 --> 23:04.760
Okay.

23:04.880 --> 23:06.580
This is a massive paradigm shift.

23:07.040 --> 23:07.780
We need a lift.

23:08.020 --> 23:14.900
We absolutely need these amplitudes to preserve memory so that systems can compose coherently over time.

23:15.060 --> 23:15.260
Right.

23:15.260 --> 23:19.400
But this brings us to a really, really interesting juncture in the math.

23:19.620 --> 23:21.040
The choice of the scalar field.

23:21.180 --> 23:21.620
Exactly.

23:22.100 --> 23:25.180
What kind of math do we actually need to build this lift?

23:25.340 --> 23:31.700
Can we just use ordinary, everyday, real numbers, the numbers we use to balance our checkbooks?

23:31.700 --> 23:34.860
It's 1, 2, negative 5, 3.14.

23:35.200 --> 23:35.440
Right.

23:35.580 --> 23:40.280
Or is the universe somehow fundamentally forcing us to use complex numbers?

23:40.820 --> 23:45.840
The numbers that include the imaginary unit i, which is the square root of negative 1.

23:45.920 --> 23:46.260
Right.

23:46.380 --> 23:49.680
The numbers that always feel like a math trick when you first learn them in high school.

23:50.200 --> 23:53.460
Why does quantum mechanics seemingly require imaginary numbers?

23:54.040 --> 23:59.660
This takes us deep into the real versus complex divide and how we map out the contexts of measurements.

23:59.660 --> 24:04.980
To figure out if we really need complex numbers, the author starts at the simplest possible level of existence.

24:05.300 --> 24:08.060
A system that only has two possible outcomes.

24:08.720 --> 24:11.600
Mathematically, we call this dimension 2 or D equals 2.

24:11.740 --> 24:15.960
Think of a coin flip or a single quantum spin up or spin down.

24:16.080 --> 24:16.280
Okay.

24:16.380 --> 24:17.100
D equals 2.

24:17.620 --> 24:18.460
Two outcomes.

24:18.680 --> 24:19.840
What happens at this level?

24:20.300 --> 24:23.580
At two outcomes, something mathematically remarkable happens.

24:23.580 --> 24:32.000
The set of all orthostochastic matrices, and these are the transition matrices generated using purely real numbers.

24:32.100 --> 24:32.320
Okay.

24:32.320 --> 24:38.640
And the set of all unistochastic matrices, which are generated using complex numbers, are mathematically identical.

24:38.760 --> 24:41.220
They form the exact same shape in the probability space.

24:41.320 --> 24:41.780
Wait, really?

24:41.880 --> 24:42.880
Let me make sure I'm hearing this.

24:42.880 --> 24:53.900
If my experiment only has two possible outcomes, a 2x2 probability table literally cannot tell you if the underlying universe is operating on real numbers or complex numbers.

24:54.080 --> 24:55.020
It absolutely cannot.

24:55.160 --> 24:56.780
They cast the exact same shadow.

24:57.440 --> 25:00.560
The author uses a brilliant visual analogy to explain why this happens.

25:00.700 --> 25:02.700
Please, because my brain needs a picture here.

25:02.840 --> 25:06.680
Imagine a perfectly spherical ball in three-dimensional space.

25:07.300 --> 25:10.040
Now, imagine a point sitting somewhere inside that ball.

25:10.120 --> 25:10.360
All right.

25:10.360 --> 25:11.820
But you have a restricted view.

25:12.260 --> 25:17.720
You're only allowed to know how far that point sits from the center of the ball along a single vertical axis.

25:17.980 --> 25:19.060
Let's call it the z's axis.

25:19.400 --> 25:19.640
Okay.

25:19.760 --> 25:22.260
So I know the point is two inches above the equator.

25:22.460 --> 25:23.180
That's all I know.

25:23.300 --> 25:23.520
Right.

25:23.780 --> 25:24.680
You know the z's coordinate.

25:25.500 --> 25:26.860
But a sphere has three dimensions.

25:27.440 --> 25:30.900
You cannot see the other two dimensions, the x-axis and the y-axis.

25:31.240 --> 25:34.220
You don't know if the point is pushed to the left or pushed forward.

25:34.440 --> 25:34.720
Yeah.

25:34.720 --> 25:44.760
Now, in this analogy, the z's axis represents the real probability, and the hidden x and y-axis represent where the real and complex math would actually diverge and disagree.

25:45.120 --> 25:45.480
Ah.

25:45.860 --> 25:49.880
So at two outcomes, the probability table is just that single z's axis.

25:50.060 --> 25:51.800
It completely underdetermines the lift.

25:51.840 --> 25:56.580
It doesn't give you enough information to even ask the question of whether the universe is real or complex.

25:56.900 --> 25:57.300
Exactly.

25:57.460 --> 25:58.480
The shadow is too narrow.

25:58.480 --> 26:00.560
But the universe isn't just binary coins.

26:00.880 --> 26:02.920
We have systems with more than two outcomes.

26:03.480 --> 26:05.600
So what happens when we add a third outcome?

26:05.840 --> 26:07.700
What happens when we hit d equals three?

26:08.040 --> 26:12.880
At three outcomes, the d equals three cliff, the coincidence completely shatters.

26:13.320 --> 26:15.460
The shadow becomes wide enough to see the difference.

26:15.560 --> 26:15.860
Okay.

26:16.160 --> 26:24.480
And the author demonstrates this using a very specific famous mathematical tool, the three-by-three discrete Fourier transform matrix.

26:24.880 --> 26:25.180
Okay.

26:25.340 --> 26:26.220
The Fourier matrix.

26:26.220 --> 26:33.320
This is a matrix that perfectly describes a very common, valid quantum transformation, right?

26:33.480 --> 26:34.340
It's used all the time.

26:34.560 --> 26:34.960
All the time.

26:35.080 --> 26:37.960
And it works beautifully, effortlessly, using complex numbers.

26:38.420 --> 26:39.280
The math is elegant.

26:40.020 --> 26:50.900
But Flyxion asks, what if we try to replicate that exact same physical transformation, that exact same scattering of probability, but we strictly forbid ourselves from using imaginary numbers?

26:51.440 --> 26:55.060
What if we try to force real numbers to do the job?

26:55.220 --> 26:55.400
Right.

26:55.400 --> 26:57.460
And what does the math say when we try to force it?

26:57.640 --> 27:07.020
The mathematics dictates that to replicate the Fourier transformation using only real numbers, you must construct what is called a three-by-three Hadamard matrix.

27:07.420 --> 27:07.660
Okay.

27:07.760 --> 27:12.200
Let's slow down and explain what a Hadamard matrix is because I actually want to try to build one in my head right now.

27:12.280 --> 27:13.500
It's conceptually very simple.

27:13.760 --> 27:19.000
A Hadamard matrix is just a square grid of numbers where the only allowed entries are positive one and negative one.

27:19.000 --> 27:24.680
And the strict rule is that every single row must be mutually orthogonal to every other row.

27:25.060 --> 27:25.980
Mutually orthogonal.

27:26.540 --> 27:35.880
Meaning if I take row one and I multiply it by row two element by element and add them up, the dot product, it has to equal exactly zero.

27:35.880 --> 27:36.360
Exactly.

27:36.360 --> 27:41.600
Geometrically, it means the rows are at perfect 90-degree right angles to each other.

27:41.740 --> 27:41.920
Okay.

27:42.060 --> 27:43.440
Let's try to build a three-by-three one.

27:43.540 --> 27:46.040
It's just a tic-tac-toe board of ones and minus ones.

27:46.280 --> 27:48.280
Let's make the first row all positive ones.

27:48.420 --> 27:49.100
One, one, one.

27:49.200 --> 27:49.480
Easy.

27:49.780 --> 27:49.960
Right.

27:49.960 --> 27:52.600
Now I need a second row that is orthogonal to it.

27:52.760 --> 27:57.760
So to make the dot product zero, I need some positive ones to cancel out the negative ones.

27:57.820 --> 27:57.920
Right.

27:58.000 --> 27:59.440
You multiply the elements and add them up.

27:59.520 --> 27:59.640
Okay.

27:59.720 --> 28:01.680
So let's try one, negative one, one.

28:01.800 --> 28:03.160
I multiply them with the first row.

28:03.400 --> 28:04.580
One times one is one.

28:04.940 --> 28:06.580
One times negative one is negative one.

28:06.660 --> 28:07.600
One times one is one.

28:07.680 --> 28:08.300
I add them up.

28:08.780 --> 28:11.360
One minus one plus one equals one.

28:11.440 --> 28:12.260
It doesn't equal zero.

28:12.360 --> 28:13.100
It doesn't equal zero.

28:13.240 --> 28:14.420
Well, let me try two negative ones.

28:14.800 --> 28:16.040
Negative one, negative one, one.

28:16.180 --> 28:17.160
Multiply with the first row.

28:17.340 --> 28:19.900
Negative one minus one plus one equals negative one.

28:20.120 --> 28:20.940
Still not zero.

28:21.100 --> 28:21.320
Nope.

28:21.780 --> 28:26.860
Wait, to get exactly zero by adding ones and negative ones, I need an equal number of positive

28:26.860 --> 28:28.440
matches and negative matches.

28:28.760 --> 28:29.420
That's exactly.

28:29.540 --> 28:31.020
But the row have three elements.

28:31.700 --> 28:32.780
Three is an odd number.

28:33.180 --> 28:35.900
You can never divide an odd number into two equal halves.

28:36.000 --> 28:37.680
You will always have one left over.

28:38.200 --> 28:42.440
And there is the stark, inescapable mathematical truth.

28:43.000 --> 28:46.060
A three by three Hadamard matrix does not exist.

28:46.240 --> 28:47.500
It is logically impossible.

28:47.800 --> 28:48.240
Wow.

28:48.240 --> 28:55.020
You can write a computer program to check all 512 possible combinations of plus and minus

28:55.020 --> 28:56.840
signs in a three by three grid.

28:57.400 --> 28:58.720
Every single one of them fails.

28:59.020 --> 29:01.780
So boom, just by thinking it through, we hit a brick wall.

29:02.280 --> 29:03.460
The real numbers fail.

29:04.280 --> 29:09.220
The universe demonstrably needs complex numbers for a single three outcome transformation.

29:09.220 --> 29:10.700
It seems that way, yes.

29:10.940 --> 29:13.860
But the author pushes even deeper into the geometry of this.

29:14.220 --> 29:18.120
Because what if we don't look at a single isolated transformation like the Fourier matrix?

29:18.320 --> 29:18.600
Okay.

29:18.760 --> 29:22.740
Where we look at a whole network of different experimental setups where every individual

29:22.740 --> 29:25.800
transformation actually works perfectly fine with real numbers.

29:25.800 --> 29:27.640
Wait, a network where the local parts work?

29:27.720 --> 29:28.860
But what, the whole thing breaks?

29:28.960 --> 29:29.220
Yes.

29:29.620 --> 29:34.380
This leads us into the territory of graph realizability and Betty numbers.

29:34.780 --> 29:35.080
Okay.

29:35.200 --> 29:36.960
Let's unpack graph realizability.

29:37.200 --> 29:38.940
How are we mapping out these experiments?

29:38.940 --> 29:43.660
We can treat different measurement contexts, like different ways of setting up your laboratory

29:43.660 --> 29:46.280
equipment, as vertices on a graph.

29:46.560 --> 29:48.700
Think of them as dots drawn on a piece of paper.

29:49.000 --> 29:49.480
Got it.

29:49.640 --> 29:55.160
And the transition probabilities between these setups, how state A scatters into state B,

29:55.400 --> 29:57.700
are the edges, the lines connecting the dots.

29:57.840 --> 30:03.620
So I have a map of all the ways my quantum system can transform from one context to another.

30:03.620 --> 30:04.060
Right.

30:04.420 --> 30:10.400
Now, if you draw these lines, connecting the dots in a way that forms what mathematicians

30:10.400 --> 30:15.220
call a spanning tree, meaning the lines branch out like a tree, but they never loop back

30:15.220 --> 30:20.080
on themselves to form a closed circle, then there is absolutely no mathematical constraint

30:20.080 --> 30:20.480
globally.

30:20.520 --> 30:21.500
Why is there no constraint?

30:21.860 --> 30:26.700
Because you can just walk down the branches of the tree, step by step, assigning real number

30:26.700 --> 30:27.860
amplitudes as you go.

30:28.140 --> 30:32.740
Because you never loop back to a dot you've already visited, you can never contradict yourself.

30:32.740 --> 30:33.560
Oh, I see.

30:33.620 --> 30:37.360
You can always find a real number lift that makes the whole tree work perfectly.

30:37.780 --> 30:38.180
I see.

30:38.220 --> 30:39.180
It's like telling a lie.

30:39.720 --> 30:43.580
As long as you keep moving forward and meeting new people, the lie holds up.

30:43.840 --> 30:48.080
But the moment you run into someone who knows the first person you lied to, you know?

30:48.080 --> 30:49.720
The contradiction is exposed.

30:50.100 --> 30:50.540
Exactly.

30:50.860 --> 30:56.160
In graph theory, the moment your graph has a cycle, a closed loop of edges, you have what

30:56.160 --> 31:00.740
is called a Betty number, denoted mathematically as beta one of one or more.

31:00.840 --> 31:01.000
Okay.

31:01.000 --> 31:03.340
And cycles create global constraints.

31:03.780 --> 31:07.720
You have to ensure that when you multiply the transitions all the way around the loop,

31:07.840 --> 31:11.160
you arrive back at your starting point without a mathematical contradiction.

31:11.520 --> 31:16.340
And this brings us to what the text calls the triangle gluing theorem and the concept of

31:16.340 --> 31:19.240
mutually unbiased bases, or MUBs.

31:19.240 --> 31:19.520
Yes.

31:19.900 --> 31:23.220
Let's imagine the simplest possible cycle, a triangle.

31:23.720 --> 31:24.700
Three measurement contexts.

31:24.800 --> 31:27.180
Let's call them context A, context B, and context C.

31:27.300 --> 31:27.540
Okay.

31:27.760 --> 31:29.200
Three dots connected in a triangle.

31:29.360 --> 31:31.860
Now imagine that the transition from A to B is perfectly fine.

31:31.920 --> 31:33.460
You can calculate it using real numbers.

31:33.580 --> 31:35.400
The transition from B to C is also fine.

31:35.940 --> 31:36.680
Real numbers work.

31:37.060 --> 31:40.760
And the transition from C back to A works with real numbers too.

31:40.760 --> 31:44.400
So locally, every individual leg of the trip is perfectly real.

31:44.540 --> 31:50.400
But Flyxion proves that for certain specific arrangements, specifically when these three

31:50.400 --> 31:55.680
contexts are mutually unbiased, meaning they are as different from each other as quantumly

31:55.680 --> 32:02.120
possible, they mathematically refuse to close if you restrict yourself globally to real numbers.

32:02.320 --> 32:04.120
Wait, I really need to make sure I grasp this.

32:04.540 --> 32:09.340
You're telling me that real numbers fail not because they are incapable of handling a single

32:09.340 --> 32:14.780
transition, but because they are incapable of making a simple triangle of transitions fit

32:14.780 --> 32:18.960
together seamlessly, even when every individual line is perfectly fine.

32:19.020 --> 32:19.420
Exactly.

32:19.960 --> 32:24.120
It is the profound difference between local existence and global compatibility.

32:24.120 --> 32:27.380
Let me give you an analogy that might make this intuitive.

32:27.620 --> 32:27.880
Please.

32:28.140 --> 32:30.980
Have you ever seen those mind-bending drawings by M.C. Escher?

32:31.320 --> 32:33.340
Like the famous endless staircase?

32:33.780 --> 32:35.540
Oh, I love that analogy, yes.

32:35.580 --> 32:39.260
Where the monks are walking up the stairs forever, but somehow they just go in a circle.

32:39.660 --> 32:39.900
Right.

32:40.100 --> 32:45.960
If you take that drawing and you cut out a small square, say, just one monk on a single

32:45.960 --> 32:49.920
step, and the step immediately in front of him, and you look at only that local section,

32:50.120 --> 32:52.760
it makes perfect three-dimensional sense.

32:52.840 --> 32:52.940
Yeah.

32:52.940 --> 32:56.340
It is a completely valid local depiction of reality.

32:56.620 --> 33:00.400
There's nothing physically impossible about one step being higher than the previous one.

33:00.520 --> 33:00.880
Exactly.

33:01.160 --> 33:06.320
But when you zoom out and you look at the entire cycle, the whole loop of the staircase, you

33:06.320 --> 33:09.220
realize they depict a globally impossible object.

33:09.440 --> 33:09.720
Right.

33:09.780 --> 33:12.400
The steps refuse to close in 3D space.

33:12.540 --> 33:12.720
Yeah.

33:12.860 --> 33:16.340
You cannot build that staircase out of wood in the real world.

33:16.560 --> 33:20.400
That is exactly what real numbers are doing in this triangle of contexts.

33:20.400 --> 33:24.680
The real numbers are locally admissible, but they are globally impossible.

33:25.080 --> 33:26.780
They create a mathematical Escher staircase.

33:27.280 --> 33:29.420
They force a contradiction when you complete the loop.

33:29.800 --> 33:30.160
Wow.

33:30.560 --> 33:35.880
Complex numbers, however, have this extra degree of rotational freedom, the phase, that I component,

33:36.500 --> 33:41.620
which gives the math enough flexibility to bend the staircase and allow the triangle to

33:41.620 --> 33:43.660
close seamlessly without contradiction.

33:43.660 --> 33:45.360
Okay, my mind is slightly blown.

33:45.840 --> 33:51.440
We have just shown that for a single isolated quantum system, if you measure it in three

33:51.440 --> 33:57.540
different ways in a loop, real numbers create an impossible paradox, but complex numbers smooth

33:57.540 --> 33:57.940
it out.

33:58.280 --> 34:00.880
This seems like a slam dunk for complex numbers.

34:00.880 --> 34:02.480
It is a very powerful argument.

34:02.480 --> 34:08.540
But everything we have talked about so far is just one single system being prodded in

34:08.540 --> 34:09.060
different ways.

34:09.160 --> 34:11.540
The universe is not just one isolated system.

34:12.060 --> 34:13.900
It's full of interacting stuff.

34:14.060 --> 34:14.300
Yes.

34:14.560 --> 34:20.260
What happens if we bring two completely separate independent quantum systems together in a room?

34:20.620 --> 34:24.020
Does the real versus complex debate change when we scale up?

34:24.280 --> 34:25.720
It changes absolutely everything.

34:25.720 --> 34:31.220
The entire paradigm shifts, and this launches us into a crucial section of Flyxion's reconstruction,

34:31.700 --> 34:34.460
joining worlds and the principle of local tomography.

34:34.840 --> 34:39.120
The problem of two systems, if I have system A over here, let's say a particle in my lab,

34:39.180 --> 34:43.600
and I have system B over there, a particle in your lab across the country, how must the mathematical

34:43.600 --> 34:46.960
descriptions of these two separate things compose into one joint description?

34:47.300 --> 34:49.260
This is a foundational question of physics.

34:50.340 --> 34:54.560
And to answer it, the author introduces a guiding principle called local tomography.

34:54.560 --> 34:59.800
Now, it is incredibly important to state up front that local tomography is an assumption.

35:00.140 --> 35:02.020
It is not a proven law of the universe.

35:02.240 --> 35:02.360
Okay.

35:02.740 --> 35:05.560
But it is a deeply physically intuitive assumption.

35:06.200 --> 35:06.280
Right.

35:06.360 --> 35:07.160
What does it assume?

35:07.660 --> 35:13.000
Local tomography assumes that the joint combined state of a composite system is exactly as informative

35:13.000 --> 35:16.960
as a complete set of local measurements on each subsystem individually.

35:17.460 --> 35:18.900
Nothing more, nothing less.

35:18.900 --> 35:25.620
Meaning, if I meticulously measure every possible property of system A in my lab, and you meticulously

35:25.620 --> 35:29.380
measure every possible property of system B in your lab, and then we get on the phone

35:29.380 --> 35:33.760
and pool our notes, we now know absolutely everything there is to know about the joint

35:33.760 --> 35:34.740
system A and B.

35:34.840 --> 35:35.100
Right.

35:35.180 --> 35:39.600
It assumes that the whole is exactly equal to the sum of its local parts.

35:40.040 --> 35:44.600
There are no spooky, hidden global variables that we can't see locally.

35:44.840 --> 35:45.060
Yeah.

35:45.060 --> 35:47.280
This is how classical physics works, by the way.

35:47.520 --> 35:51.320
If I know the state of every individual gear in a watch, I know the state of the entire

35:51.320 --> 35:51.760
watch.

35:51.920 --> 35:53.380
It seems like common sense.

35:53.880 --> 35:56.240
So, we assume local tomography.

35:56.840 --> 36:00.020
How does this help us decide between real and complex numbers?

36:00.780 --> 36:06.440
We cast the number systems against this assumption using a brilliant mathematical trick called a

36:06.440 --> 36:07.760
dimension counting argument.

36:08.160 --> 36:11.940
This was originally developed by physicists Araki, Wooders, and Hardy.

36:11.940 --> 36:17.000
Essentially, we literally just count the number of parameters, the number of dials needed

36:17.000 --> 36:18.900
to fully describe the states.

36:19.120 --> 36:20.080
Let's run the numbers then.

36:20.200 --> 36:23.000
Let's start with our reigning champion, the complex numbers.

36:23.080 --> 36:23.280
Okay.

36:23.360 --> 36:29.160
Let's say a single quantum system has a dimension of D. To fully describe its state using complex

36:29.160 --> 36:33.120
numbers, the math requires exactly D squared parameters.

36:33.240 --> 36:33.440
Okay.

36:33.540 --> 36:34.140
D squared.

36:34.140 --> 36:39.460
Now, if we have two independent systems, A and B, and we measure them locally, the number

36:39.460 --> 36:44.900
of parameters is D squared for A multiplied by D squared for B. That gives us D to the fourth

36:44.900 --> 36:46.240
total local parameters.

36:46.320 --> 36:46.460
Right.

36:46.720 --> 36:48.580
D squared times D squared is it to the fourth.

36:48.740 --> 36:50.180
Now, we flip the perspective.

36:50.840 --> 36:56.220
What if we look at the composite system A and B directly as one big complex object and

36:56.220 --> 36:59.360
calculate its dimension using the complex math rules?

36:59.360 --> 37:03.940
The formula for the composite space also yields exactly D to the fourth parameters.

37:04.080 --> 37:04.400
Wow.

37:04.720 --> 37:06.560
It is a perfect exact match.

37:06.880 --> 37:09.820
The local parameters perfectly add up to the global parameters.

37:10.440 --> 37:14.000
Local tomography holds beautifully and effortlessly for complex numbers.

37:14.240 --> 37:14.660
It does.

37:15.000 --> 37:16.880
Now, let's test a different number system.

37:17.060 --> 37:18.040
Let's look at the quaternions.

37:18.280 --> 37:19.780
The 4D number system.

37:20.200 --> 37:23.960
A lot of physicists wonder if quaternions could describe quantum mechanics.

37:24.440 --> 37:25.040
They do.

37:25.040 --> 37:27.400
But watch what happens when we count the dimensions.

37:28.400 --> 37:31.160
If you use quaternions, the math undershoots.

37:31.920 --> 37:36.300
The naive product of the local measurements gives you a number of parameters that is actually

37:36.300 --> 37:40.060
larger than what the composite system's math can physically hold.

37:41.000 --> 37:42.720
The local view is overcomplete.

37:42.980 --> 37:43.920
It doesn't map neatly.

37:44.340 --> 37:44.580
Okay.

37:44.760 --> 37:46.100
So quaternions are awkward.

37:46.540 --> 37:49.860
But what about our familiar, stubborn, real numbers?

37:50.120 --> 37:51.540
How do they handle two systems?

37:51.740 --> 37:52.980
This is where the shock happens.

37:52.980 --> 37:55.520
For real numbers, the math overshoots.

37:56.140 --> 38:01.200
A real number composite system has more joint parameters than local measurements can possibly

38:01.200 --> 38:01.900
account for.

38:02.220 --> 38:04.460
I need an example here because this sounds like magic.

38:05.100 --> 38:07.600
Let's look at the simplest possible two-part system.

38:07.880 --> 38:08.500
Two ribbits.

38:08.940 --> 38:14.420
Or, since we're forcing ourselves to use real numbers, physicists call them rebits.

38:14.960 --> 38:15.480
Real bit.

38:15.560 --> 38:15.840
Perfect.

38:16.180 --> 38:21.460
A single rebit, mathematically, requires exactly three parameters to describe its state fully.

38:21.460 --> 38:21.860
Okay.

38:22.060 --> 38:23.480
Three dials on my local machine.

38:23.900 --> 38:28.460
So, if you have two completely independent rebits, Alice has one and Bob has one, the

38:28.460 --> 38:32.800
local parts should require three times three, which is nine parameters in total.

38:32.960 --> 38:33.260
Right.

38:33.700 --> 38:34.920
Alice gives me her three numbers.

38:35.100 --> 38:36.260
Bob gives me his three numbers.

38:36.360 --> 38:36.920
I have nine numbers.

38:37.020 --> 38:37.720
I should know everything.

38:37.900 --> 38:44.560
But when you calculate the mathematical dimension of the composite state space of two rebits acting

38:44.560 --> 38:47.580
together, the linear algebra formula gives you ten.

38:47.920 --> 38:48.220
Ten?

38:48.320 --> 38:49.880
Where does the tenth dimension come from?

38:49.980 --> 38:51.460
I only have nine local dials.

38:51.540 --> 38:52.960
The math says it has to be there.

38:53.200 --> 38:55.060
It's like, um, okay, imagine a couple.

38:55.320 --> 38:55.880
Alice and Bob.

38:56.160 --> 38:56.880
You go to Alice.

38:57.060 --> 38:58.080
You interview her alone.

38:58.200 --> 39:00.280
She tells you her three main personality traits.

39:00.480 --> 39:01.420
You go to Bob alone.

39:01.500 --> 39:02.740
He tells you his three traits.

39:03.080 --> 39:06.400
You think you have nine data points that describe them completely.

39:06.400 --> 39:11.340
But when Alice and Bob are actually in a room together, a tenth dynamic emerges.

39:12.220 --> 39:18.280
A secret inside joke, or a specific way they argue, that is totally invisible to anyone

39:18.280 --> 39:20.180
who only talks to them individually.

39:20.440 --> 39:21.360
That's a great way to put it.

39:21.440 --> 39:26.220
They possess a joint reality that cannot be constructed from their individual interviews.

39:26.600 --> 39:30.120
That is an incredibly accurate characterization of the math.

39:30.640 --> 39:33.720
But I must emphasize again a point Flyxion makes strongly.

39:33.720 --> 39:36.540
Local tomography is an assumption.

39:37.400 --> 39:39.280
It is not a forced law of nature.

39:39.500 --> 39:39.700
Right.

39:39.840 --> 39:41.960
The universe doesn't have to obey our common sense.

39:42.300 --> 39:42.760
Exactly.

39:43.640 --> 39:48.300
If we aggressively assume that the whole must equal the sum of the local measurements,

39:48.820 --> 39:54.080
if we demand that there are no secret inside jokes, then complex numbers are the unique

39:54.080 --> 39:55.460
mathematical fixed point.

39:55.580 --> 39:58.120
They're the only number system that works without contradiction.

39:58.360 --> 40:00.280
Because they perfectly match to the fourth.

40:00.280 --> 40:04.900
But if we don't assume local tomography, if we are willing to accept that the whole might

40:04.900 --> 40:09.900
be greater than the parts, then this dimension mismatch for real numbers isn't just a mathematical

40:09.900 --> 40:10.340
error.

40:10.780 --> 40:12.360
It is a physical hiding place.

40:12.640 --> 40:13.540
A hiding place.

40:14.180 --> 40:15.620
That gives me absolute chills.

40:16.040 --> 40:21.560
Which naturally makes me ask, what exactly is hiding in that invisible tenth dimension if

40:21.560 --> 40:22.680
we use real numbers?

40:22.920 --> 40:24.700
And what happens when we try to look at it?

40:24.700 --> 40:30.420
Flyxion's text explicitly identifies the mathematical nature of this missing tenth dimension.

40:31.200 --> 40:37.720
It is the direction in the state space spanned by a mathematical operator called J tensor J.

40:37.920 --> 40:38.240
Okay.

40:38.560 --> 40:39.160
Jargon alert.

40:39.300 --> 40:40.040
Let's break that down.

40:40.160 --> 40:40.700
What is J?

40:40.820 --> 40:43.960
J is the antisymmetric quarter-turn generator.

40:44.660 --> 40:49.860
In plain English, it is just a simple two-by-two matrix that rotates a state by exactly 90 degrees.

40:49.980 --> 40:50.140
Okay.

40:50.260 --> 40:51.040
A quarter-turn.

40:51.180 --> 40:52.640
Why is it called antisymmetric?

40:52.640 --> 40:56.880
Antisymmetric means that if you flip the matrix across its main diagonal, all the positive

40:56.880 --> 40:59.520
numbers become negative, and the negatives become positive.

40:59.600 --> 41:00.020
Oh, I see.

41:00.060 --> 41:01.520
A rotation matrix has this property.

41:01.660 --> 41:06.040
If you rotate 90 degrees right, the opposite is rotating 90 degrees left, which is negative.

41:06.160 --> 41:06.640
Makes sense.

41:07.100 --> 41:09.260
But here's the crazy part that the book mentions.

41:10.060 --> 41:15.520
A single J matrix on its own is not a valid observable property in the real number theory.

41:15.980 --> 41:20.340
You cannot build a machine in your lab to locally measure J on a single rebit.

41:20.340 --> 41:20.860
Correct.

41:21.360 --> 41:23.320
It is locally inadmissible.

41:23.760 --> 41:25.580
It's invisible to local observation.

41:26.020 --> 41:29.820
But look what happens when we tensor them together to describe the joint system.

41:30.660 --> 41:32.160
J tensor J.

41:32.540 --> 41:35.180
Tensoring is just multiplying the spaces together, right?

41:35.220 --> 41:35.480
Yes.

41:36.020 --> 41:41.420
And when you multiply an antisymmetric matrix by another antisymmetric matrix, the two negatives

41:41.420 --> 41:42.800
mathematically cancel out.

41:43.240 --> 41:44.940
A negative times a negative is a positive.

41:44.940 --> 41:48.820
The composite matrix, J tensor J, becomes symmetric.

41:49.480 --> 41:52.260
And in quantum mechanics, symmetric matrices are...

41:52.260 --> 41:53.500
They're valid observables.

41:53.720 --> 41:54.040
Whoa.

41:54.540 --> 41:59.700
So an operation that is mathematically impossible to observe on a single particle suddenly becomes

41:59.700 --> 42:03.960
a completely valid physical property when you look at two particles together.

42:04.100 --> 42:04.500
Exactly.

42:04.660 --> 42:07.200
It's locally inadmissible, but globally admissible.

42:07.340 --> 42:07.660
Exactly.

42:07.800 --> 42:09.040
That is the secret 10th dimension.

42:09.540 --> 42:10.400
But there is a catch.

42:10.400 --> 42:13.280
A dimension in math is just a line, right?

42:13.360 --> 42:14.540
It can stretch off to infinity.

42:15.160 --> 42:17.700
Can this hidden dynamic just be infinitely strong?

42:18.100 --> 42:21.700
I would assume no, because physics usually breaks if things go to infinity.

42:21.960 --> 42:25.420
It doesn't go to infinity because of the foundational requirement of positivity.

42:25.760 --> 42:28.360
Positivity meaning probabilities can't be negative.

42:28.780 --> 42:29.000
Right.

42:29.220 --> 42:32.360
You cannot have a negative 20% chance of a detector clicking.

42:32.580 --> 42:33.460
It's nonsensical.

42:33.460 --> 42:41.300
Because the total probabilities must remain positive, this hidden J tensor J direction is strictly bounded.

42:41.980 --> 42:48.060
It supports a genuine physical family of states that are controlled by a hidden parameter, which we can call C.

42:48.720 --> 42:52.680
And the math dictates that C must be strictly between negative 1 and 1.

42:52.800 --> 42:57.260
So we have this hidden dial C that we can turn up and down between negative 1 and 1.

42:57.260 --> 43:00.660
It is a real physical interval of joint states.

43:01.120 --> 43:07.120
What happens if I set C to 0.5, but then I try to look at it by just measuring one of the particles locally?

43:07.560 --> 43:11.360
Let's say Alice and Bob are on different planets, and Alice measures her rebit.

43:11.560 --> 43:16.600
The mathematical process for ignoring one subsystem to look at the other is called the partial trace.

43:16.740 --> 43:17.640
The partial trace.

43:17.740 --> 43:17.940
Yes.

43:17.940 --> 43:26.160
If you perform a partial trace on this J tensor J state, meaning you mathematically trace out Bob's particle and only look at Alice's local view,

43:26.620 --> 43:30.960
the C parameter completely unequivocally vanishes from the equation.

43:31.200 --> 43:32.840
It drops to absolute zero.

43:33.080 --> 43:35.000
So Alice sees nothing of the hidden dial?

43:35.380 --> 43:35.680
Nothing.

43:36.520 --> 43:45.080
Both marginals Alice's local view and Bob's local view look exactly identical to what we call the maximally mixed state.

43:45.080 --> 43:48.120
Meaning total uniform randomness.

43:48.480 --> 43:51.240
The dead center of the probability triangle we talked about earlier.

43:51.540 --> 43:56.420
Alice just sees a coin flipping 50-50, pure noise, no structure at all.

43:56.560 --> 43:56.900
Exactly.

43:57.060 --> 44:00.440
The structure is globally physical, but locally completely invisible.

44:01.020 --> 44:05.320
It is hidden in the relations between the particles, not in the particles themselves.

44:05.440 --> 44:06.780
Okay, this is fascinating.

44:06.900 --> 44:10.160
But here is the twist in the text that absolutely blew my mind.

44:10.200 --> 44:12.340
I had to reread this page three times.

44:12.340 --> 44:16.040
Let's talk about entanglement, the famous spooky action at a distance.

44:16.320 --> 44:16.680
Ah, yes.

44:16.680 --> 44:22.660
In this real number framework, if that secret dial C is turned up to anything other than zero, is the state entangled?

44:22.900 --> 44:23.240
Yes.

44:24.060 --> 44:26.400
Mathematically, it fails the separability test.

44:26.680 --> 44:32.420
It cannot be written as a simple classical probability mixture of Alice's local states and Bob's local states.

44:32.620 --> 44:34.760
It is formally mathematically entangled.

44:34.760 --> 44:48.100
But you, and this is a massive but, if you switch your mathematical toolkit, if you say, okay, let's stop using real numbers and I allow you to use complex numbers instead, what happens to that exact same physical state?

44:48.240 --> 45:01.040
That exact same family of states describing the exact same physical reality in the lab can suddenly be written out mathematically as a perfectly separable, unentangled, convex combination.

45:01.200 --> 45:03.940
Let that sink in for a second because it is staggering.

45:03.940 --> 45:06.880
It forces a complete philosophical re-evaluation.

45:07.360 --> 45:15.460
You're saying that a quantum state is entangled or not, depending entirely on which mathematical number system we choose to do our accounting with on paper.

45:15.620 --> 45:16.240
Basically, yes.

45:16.500 --> 45:21.120
Entanglement isn't some deep, physical, spooky wire connecting the particles themselves.

45:21.280 --> 45:23.020
It's just an artifact of our math.

45:23.200 --> 45:25.800
The author walks through this profound implication very carefully.

45:26.000 --> 45:26.140
Yeah.

45:26.140 --> 45:33.100
Separability, the lack of entanglement, is not an intrinsic, God-given property of a physical state matrix alone.

45:33.600 --> 45:41.080
It is a property of a state relative to the scalar field, real versus complex, over which we are allowed to build our local product states.

45:41.420 --> 45:46.160
Meaning, by upgrading our math to complex numbers, we give ourselves a bigger toolkit.

45:46.160 --> 45:47.100
Precisely.

45:47.100 --> 45:55.020
By switching to complex numbers, you expand the definition of what counts as a valid local state because you have phase to play with.

45:55.160 --> 45:55.680
Oh, wow.

45:55.940 --> 46:04.860
And with that expanded toolkit, you can suddenly build that seemingly impossible J tensor J state purely out of local, unentangled parts.

46:04.860 --> 46:13.140
We are exposing entanglement, the most mystical part of quantum mechanics, as a structural consequence of our mathematical rigidity.

46:13.320 --> 46:13.660
Exactly.

46:13.960 --> 46:16.660
It's not that the universe is spooky and non-local.

46:16.960 --> 46:23.800
It's that our real number math was too restricted to properly describe how things compose so it looked like the particles were magically connected.

46:24.320 --> 46:30.180
When you upgrade the grammar to complex numbers, the magic just becomes regular local composition.

46:30.180 --> 46:37.080
It is a stunning demystification, but this raises an incredibly important question for the rest of the text.

46:37.080 --> 46:50.320
If Flyxion can derive interference just from probability projection, and if they can derive geometry from graph constraints, and if they can reveal entanglement as merely relative to our choice of scalar field,

46:50.320 --> 47:00.200
what does it take to get the rest of the classic quantum mechanics textbook, the heavy lifting, the equations physicists use every day?

47:00.640 --> 47:03.340
How much do we have to assume to get the rest of the physics?

47:03.400 --> 47:06.040
Because usually in physics, you have to postulate a lot of things.

47:06.120 --> 47:09.200
You say, assume this equation is true, and let's see what happens.

47:09.380 --> 47:09.580
Yes.

47:09.860 --> 47:13.580
Flyxion actually provides an assumption ledger near the end of the text.

47:13.580 --> 47:22.180
To derive the entire standard, incredibly successful framework of quantum mechanics, how many postulates did they actually need?

47:22.460 --> 47:23.680
It is remarkably lean.

47:24.180 --> 47:26.440
Exactly four explicitly stated assumptions.

47:26.700 --> 47:29.480
Only four assumptions to get the entire quantum universe.

47:29.680 --> 47:31.180
Let's walk through them one by one.

47:31.260 --> 47:31.920
Assumption one.

47:32.340 --> 47:33.120
Local tomography.

47:33.180 --> 47:33.400
Right.

47:33.480 --> 47:34.780
We just spent a lot of time on this.

47:34.840 --> 47:34.940
Yeah.

47:34.940 --> 47:37.940
We assume the whole is equal to the sum of the local measurements.

47:38.200 --> 47:40.380
We assume no hidden tenth dimensions.

47:40.860 --> 47:42.160
It's an assumption, not a proof.

47:42.700 --> 47:43.140
Exactly.

47:43.580 --> 47:44.280
Assumption two.

47:45.020 --> 47:49.100
Pure states are what the text calls frame-completable vectors.

47:49.400 --> 47:50.620
Frame-completable vectors.

47:50.720 --> 47:52.340
What does that mean in physical terms?

47:52.760 --> 47:54.100
It's an operational assumption.

47:54.100 --> 48:00.840
It basically means that a state can always be treated as one single column in a larger matrix of possibilities.

48:01.760 --> 48:02.740
Think of a compass needle.

48:03.200 --> 48:12.740
No matter which way the needle is pointing, that's your pure state, you can always draw a full 360-degree coordinate frame around it where that needle is one of the axes.

48:12.740 --> 48:13.080
Okay.

48:13.280 --> 48:17.100
So every state fits neatly into a larger map of possibilities.

48:17.220 --> 48:17.360
Right.

48:17.560 --> 48:18.160
Assumption three.

48:19.080 --> 48:21.220
Measurement outcomes are labeled by real numbers.

48:21.560 --> 48:27.100
Which is just saying that when I look at the detector, it spits out a real number like spin-up or five electron volts.

48:27.100 --> 48:30.120
It doesn't spit out three plus four imaginary volts.

48:30.120 --> 48:31.780
Our dials read real numbers.

48:31.780 --> 48:33.700
A very safe empirical assumption.

48:34.120 --> 48:35.240
And finally, assumption four.

48:36.100 --> 48:39.500
Time evolution is a strongly continuous unitary group.

48:39.500 --> 48:40.740
Let's translate that.

48:41.080 --> 48:43.780
Time evolution means how the system changes over time.

48:44.240 --> 48:45.920
Continuous means it flows smoothly.

48:46.320 --> 48:49.660
It doesn't jump instantly from state A to state B in zero seconds.

48:49.860 --> 48:49.940
Yep.

48:50.200 --> 48:55.260
And unitary, as we said before, means it is perfectly reversible and preserves probabilities.

48:55.500 --> 48:55.740
Yes.

48:56.280 --> 48:58.540
Smooth, reversible change over time.

48:58.660 --> 48:59.560
Those are the four assumptions.

48:59.560 --> 49:08.840
And from these four incredibly basic logical statements alone, almost everything else in the textbook is derived mathematically, not postulated physically.

49:09.020 --> 49:09.420
Show me.

49:09.640 --> 49:15.720
Because the most famous equation in quantum mechanics, the one printed on t-shirts, is the Schrodinger equation.

49:15.840 --> 49:16.080
Mm-hmm.

49:16.200 --> 49:17.700
Where does that come from in this ledger?

49:17.900 --> 49:20.880
It comes directly and inescapably from assumption four.

49:21.140 --> 49:24.460
From just assuming time is continuous and reversible.

49:24.940 --> 49:25.300
How?

49:25.300 --> 49:27.980
The Schrodinger equation has energy in it.

49:28.180 --> 49:31.680
Where does energy come from if we were just talking about continuous time?

49:32.020 --> 49:33.080
This is the beauty of the math.

49:33.420 --> 49:47.500
If you assume that time evolution is a continuous reversible group, the mathematics of linear algebra, specifically something called Stone's theorem on one parameter unitary groups, automatically hands you a mathematical object called a generator.

49:47.720 --> 49:50.980
A generator, like an engine that pushes the state forward in time.

49:51.340 --> 49:51.760
Exactly.

49:51.760 --> 49:58.880
The math dictates that the state must evolve infinitesimally according to a very specific equation.

50:00.060 --> 50:05.840
E times the derivative of the state with respect to time equals this generator multiplied by the state.

50:05.980 --> 50:07.060
Let's call the generator H.

50:07.280 --> 50:10.160
E times derivative of state equals H times state.

50:10.280 --> 50:12.340
Wait, that is the Schrodinger equation?

50:12.560 --> 50:14.220
It is the exact architectural spline of it.

50:14.300 --> 50:18.720
It emerges purely as the infinitesimal form of continuous reversible composition.

50:19.440 --> 50:21.160
You don't need to know what energy is.

50:21.160 --> 50:24.080
The math just demands a generator H exists.

50:24.300 --> 50:25.020
That is staggering.

50:25.320 --> 50:29.340
The math forces the equation into existence just to preserve information.

50:29.340 --> 50:29.820
Yes.

50:30.320 --> 50:41.820
Now, identifying that abstract mathematical generator H as the physical concept of energy and identifying the scale factor to make the units work out as Planck's constant, those are physical empirical choices that physicists added later.

50:41.860 --> 50:42.440
Oh, well, I see.

50:42.560 --> 50:45.520
Usually by looking at how quantum mechanics scales up to classical mechanics.

50:45.920 --> 50:52.320
But the architecture of the equation itself, it is strictly entirely about preserving information over continuous time.

50:52.320 --> 50:53.600
Okay, that blows my mind.

50:53.600 --> 50:55.640
What about observables?

50:56.240 --> 51:10.940
We are taught in physics one that observables, things we can measure like momentum or position, have to be represented by Hermitian operators, which is a fancy mathematical term that basically guarantees the operator will spit out real eigenvalues.

51:10.940 --> 51:13.360
That is not a mysterious quantum law.

51:13.620 --> 51:17.600
It is just a direct mathematical consequence of assumption three.

51:17.860 --> 51:20.200
That measurement outcomes are real numbers.

51:20.300 --> 51:20.820
Exactly.

51:21.000 --> 51:28.820
If you insist on assigning real numbers to sharp, distinct measurements, there is a bedrock principle in linear algebra called the spectral theorem.

51:29.020 --> 51:29.240
Right.

51:29.240 --> 51:38.280
The spectral theorem guarantees that any mathematical object that spits out real orthogonal values in this way is, by definition, a Hermitian operator.

51:38.680 --> 51:41.460
It's a mathematical equivalence, not a law of physics.

51:41.660 --> 51:44.220
The universe isn't forcing us to use Hermitian operators.

51:44.480 --> 51:47.740
Our desire for real number readouts forces us to use them.

51:47.900 --> 51:48.380
Precisely.

51:48.460 --> 51:53.160
And finally, there is this concept in the text called Nymark dilation, which deals with POVMs.

51:53.260 --> 51:54.620
Let's define POVM first.

51:54.620 --> 51:59.120
It stands for Positive Operator Value to Measure, which is a terrible name.

51:59.440 --> 51:59.560
Yeah.

51:59.940 --> 52:03.540
But it basically means a fuzzy or generalized measurement, right?

52:03.580 --> 52:06.600
